        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are smart dehumidifiers worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are worth it when the unit sits somewhere you do not visit daily, runs on its bucket, or you want to track humidity over time. The bucket-full alert and the remote humidity reading are the useful features. If the unit drains by hose to a floor drain in a space you walk through every day, the app adds little.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do smart dehumidifiers work on 5 GHz Wi-Fi?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost never. The Wi-Fi modules in these units are 2.4 GHz only. TCL's support pages say so directly, and Electrolux, Frigidaire's parent, gives the same requirement for its connected air purifiers. On a dual-band router, connect your phone to the 2.4 GHz band during setup or give that band its own network name.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What app does a Midea smart dehumidifier use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Midea's current Cube dehumidifiers use the MSmartHome app, which Midea's US product pages call the SmartHome app, with Alexa and Google Home support. Some older Midea units and documents refer to the Midea Air app. Use whichever app the quick-start card in the box names, since Midea has changed apps over time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which smart dehumidifier works with Alexa and Google?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Frigidaire FHDD5034Y1 and FHDD2234W1, TCL H50D44W and H50D46W, and the Midea Cube MAD50PS1QWT all list both Alexa and Google support, and GE's ADHE50WWF listing adds both. Honeywell's smart units have historically listed Alexa only. LG's DT501BKR0 product page lists ThinQ app control but does not name the voice assistants it supports.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a smart dehumidifier still work without Wi-Fi?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every unit here has a full control panel, a built-in humidistat and auto restart, so it dries the air normally without a network. You lose the remote humidity reading, the bucket alerts and app scheduling, but the dehumidifier itself keeps working exactly like a non-smart unit, including after a power outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the TCL 50 pint smart dehumidifier any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On paper it is one of the better values. The H50D44W's ENERGY STAR record lists 49.66 pints a day under the current test at 2.01 liters per kWh, the same efficiency as the Frigidaire, with TCL Home app control, bucket alerts and Alexa and Google support. The H50D46W adds a built-in pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a smart dehumidifier save energy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a little by itself. The humidistat already stops the compressor at the target, so the main saving comes from choosing a sensible setting, about 50% in a basement. A schedule that keeps the unit off at night saves some power, and the app makes it easy to spot a unit that runs constantly because the space has a water problem.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for Wi-Fi on a hose-drained unit you see daily</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the dehumidifier drains by hose to a floor drain in a basement you walk through every day, the bucket never fills and the display already shows the humidity. The app adds little. Put the money toward a better unit, a pump model or a second dehumidifier for the far end of the basement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to pair on a 5 GHz network</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nearly every smart dehumidifier uses a 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi module and cannot see 5 GHz. If pairing times out, give the 2.4 GHz band its own network name on the router, connect your phone to it for setup, or temporarily disable 5 GHz. A mesh node near the basement stairs fixes later dropouts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the bucket alert as a drainage plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An alert that the bucket is full still means carrying 15 to 20 pounds of water upstairs, two or three times a day in a damp summer. In a basement, use a hose to a floor drain or buy a pump model, and let the app watch the humidity instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a "smart 50 pint" that is really a 30</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some smart units still sold as 50 pints, such as Honeywell's TP50AWKN, are rated around 30 to 32 pints under the 2019 test. Check the DOE standard on the listing or the ENERGY STAR product finder, which always shows the current figure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on a voice routine as the main control</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Routines that switch the dehumidifier using another sensor depend on two clouds working together and can break silently after an update. Keep the dehumidifier's own humidistat set to about 50% as the primary control, so the unit keeps drying whatever the app or assistant does.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

